# Authoring Style for Source Rules

This rule fires whenever an author opens or edits a file under `source/**/*.md`. It is the
"eat-our-own-dog-food" guidance the central repo uses on itself. The fuller, prose-form authoring
guide lands in `docs/authoring-guide.md` in Phase 3; this MDC file is the always-loaded checklist.

Reference: `docs/02-implementation-plan.md` §6 (Phase 3) and `docs/01-architecture-upgrade-report.md`
§5.1 + Appendix A.

## 1. Mandatory Section Structure

Every source rule MUST contain these three top-level sections, in this order, after the YAML
frontmatter:

```markdown
# <Title — same as frontmatter `title`>

## 1. Context & Architectural Intent
<Why this rule exists. The architectural problem it prevents. 2–6 paragraphs.>

## 2. Enforced Standards (AI Ingestion Core)
### 2.1. <First enforced standard>
* **Rule**: <one sentence, imperative>
* **Rationale**: <one paragraph>
* **Implementation Requirement**:
  ```<lang>
  // ❌ ANTI-PATTERN
  ...
  // ✅ CORRECT
  ...
  ```

### 2.2. <Second enforced standard>
... (same shape)

## 3. AI Directives
<Imperative bullet list aimed at the agent. "When generating <X>, you MUST <Y>.">
```

## 2. Mandatory Paired ❌ / ✅ Convention

Every enforced standard in §2 MUST contain at least one paired example with the literal markers
`❌ ANTI-PATTERN` and `✅ CORRECT`. The paired form is what makes the rule legible to a downstream
LLM: a single side (only "do" or only "don't") halves the signal.

```markdown
// ❌ ANTI-PATTERN
@PostMapping("/users")
public UserResponse create(@RequestBody UserCreateRequest req) { ... }

// ✅ CORRECT
@PostMapping("/users")
public UserResponse create(@Valid @RequestBody UserCreateRequest req) { ... }
```

Both blocks MUST use the same language fence and be minimal — only the lines that demonstrate the
contrast. Resist the urge to inline imports, package declarations, or unrelated boilerplate; they
dilute the signal.

## 3. When `alwaysApply: true` Is Appropriate (Rare)

Default to `cursor_mode: auto-attach` with a layer-resolved glob. Choose `alwaysApply: true` (which
maps to `cursor_mode: always` in source frontmatter) ONLY when:

- The rule is **architectural** and cross-cuts every file (e.g., `source/_global/clean-architecture.md`).
- The rule is **safety-critical** and applies regardless of file type (e.g., "never log secrets").
- A reviewer has explicitly approved the choice in the PR description.

Excessive `alwaysApply: true` rules erode the token-budget gain that motivated the
layer-keyed split in the first place. See `docs/02-implementation-plan.md` §14 R-06.

## 4. Dependency References

Use `dependencies: [<source-rule-id>, ...]` in the frontmatter to declare prerequisites. The
compiler topologically sorts dependencies so they are emitted first when concatenating into the
single-file targets (Copilot, Claude, Junie). A cycle fails the build.

Cite the architecture report and ADRs in the prose body using relative links:
`[ADR-0002](../../../docs/decision-records/0002-layer-keyed-source-split.md)`.

## 5. ArchUnit Test References

If the rule can be expressed as a static-analysis check, add an `archunit_test:` pointer in the
frontmatter even if the test file does not exist yet — Phase 6 will fill them in. The path is
relative to the source-tree root and looks like `testing/archunit/ControllerValidationTest.java`.

## 6. Quick Checklist Before Saving

- [ ] Frontmatter parses as YAML and contains every required field (see `frontmatter-spec.mdc`).
- [ ] Title in the H1 matches `title:` in the frontmatter.
- [ ] At least one paired ❌/✅ block per enforced standard.
- [ ] `dependencies:` lists IDs that actually exist under `source/`.
- [ ] `archunit_test:` points to a real path OR is omitted (not pointed at vapor).
- [ ] No `alwaysApply: true` without justification in the PR description.

## 7. AI Directives

- When asked to draft a new rule, START from the structure in §1; do not invent alternative shapes.
- When asked to add a "do this" example, ALWAYS pair it with the corresponding "don't do this".
- When asked to set `alwaysApply: true`, push back unless the rule is in `source/_global/` or has
  `layers: [architecture]`.
- When unsure whether a rule belongs in `_global/` or under a specific layer, prefer the specific
  layer — the layer-keyed split (ADR-0002) exists exactly to avoid cross-cutting bloat.
